<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lugus was a god of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, known from inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and elsewhere, sometimes in the plural form Lugoves. His name survives in place names such as Lugdunum, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and he is widely linked with the Irish Lugh and the Welsh Lleu. Many scholars think he was the Gaulish god whom Caesar identified with Mercury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugus.jpg" alt="Lugus" width="320" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Talskubilos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PenalbaDeVillastar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
